{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n`rebar reopen <child>` moves a closed ticket to open but does NOT cascade to its\nstill-closed parent(s). `reopen` is a thin delegation to\n`transition(ticket_id, \"closed\", \"open\")` (`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:430`; CLI\n`reopen_cli` at `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:689`), and the parent-first cascade in\n`transition_compute` fires only on the `open -> in_progress` edge (`_cascade_open_parent`,\ndefined at `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:387`, called from `transition_compute` at\n`src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:341`). The close-time guard only blocks CLOSING a\nparent that has open children, so nothing catches the other direction: the store can hold\na CLOSED parent with an OPEN/in_progress child.\n\nObserved while closing epic e215-ab23-ed6a-4791: after the epic returned to `closed`,\nreopening child stories aabf-d89e-d05a-45c4 and 075b-1479-dcb7-40ab left the epic `closed`\nwhile the children were `in_progress`.\n\n## Approach\nGeneralize the existing cascade seam rather than adding a second parallel one, so both\nedges keep one implementation, one error shape, and one cycle guard.\n\n1. Add `_resolve_parent_in_status(tracker, ticket_id, status)` beside\n   `_resolve_open_parent` (`src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:93`), which today hardcodes\n   the `\"open\"` comparison. `_resolve_open_parent` becomes a one-line delegation, so its\n   other caller (`src/rebar/_commands/claim.py:190,193,215`) needs no edit.\n2. Replace `_cascade_open_parent` with an edge-table-driven `_cascade_parent_first`: a\n   module-level map from `(current_status, target_status)` to the eligible parent status —\n   `(\"open\", \"in_progress\") -> \"open\"` (today's behavior, unchanged) and\n   `(\"closed\", \"open\") -> \"closed\"` (the fix). The parent transitions on the SAME edge\n   before the child, recursing up the chain under the existing `_cascade_seen` cycle guard\n   with the existing fail-fast wrapping that names the parent and preserves\n   `ConcurrencyMismatch` (exit 10). Update the call site at\n   `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:341` and the stale references to the old name: the\n   comment above that call site and the `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py:13`\n   module docstring.\n3. Close the cascade's TOCTOU asymmetry with `claim`. The cascade DECISION reads the\n   parent's status without the write lock, so a peer can move it in between.\n   `claim_compute` already re-checks the parent's live status on a parent failure and\n   treats an already-moved parent as benign (`src/rebar/_commands/claim.py:215`);\n   `_cascade_open_parent` does not, so a benign race aborts the child. Give\n   `_cascade_parent_first` the same re-check. This DOES loosen the existing\n   `open -> in_progress` failure path (a benign race lets the child through instead of\n   aborting) — intended parity with `claim`, and only where the cascade's purpose was\n   already satisfied.\n4. Update the `reopen` docstring (`src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:430`), the\n   `transition_compute` docstring, and `docs/concurrency.md` §I4a, which currently states\n   that `close`/`reopen`/`blocked` never cascade.\n5. Repair `test_child_closure_does_not_recurse_grandchildren` in\n   `tests/interfaces/store/test_completion_verifier.py`. It manufactures a\n   closed-child-with-open-grandchild state by reopening the grandchild after its ancestors\n   closed — exactly the invalid state this bug is about — so the new cascade dissolves its\n   precondition. Rebuild that state with the documented `cascade=False` replay seam (still\n   reachable in the field via an offline clone's merge or an NDJSON import), leaving the\n   test's subject and assertion untouched.\n\nWhy this fits: the invariant restored here (\"a parent is never more closed than its\nchildren\") is symmetric with the one `open -> in_progress` already enforces, and the\nexisting helper already carries the fail-fast, exit-code-preserving, cycle-guarded\nsemantics this edge needs. A separate copy would be a second place to drift.\n\nSemantics deliberately MIRROR the documented cascade (`docs/concurrency.md` §I4a): one\ninvocation, sequential and fail-fast, NOT transactional — a genuine parent failure aborts\nthe child preserving its exit code, and a parent that succeeded before a child failure is\nnot rolled back. Only `closed -> open` is ADDED to the edge table; `* -> closed` keeps its\nnon-cascading behavior and its open-children guard, and a parent that is\n`in_progress`/`blocked`/`open`/absent is not cascaded.\n\n## Scope\n- `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py` — add `_resolve_parent_in_status`, reduce\n  `_resolve_open_parent` to a delegation, replace `_cascade_open_parent` with\n  `_cascade_parent_first` plus the benign-race re-check, update call site and docstrings.\n- `src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py` — refresh the module docstring's reference to\n  the renamed helper.\n- `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py` — document the reopen cascade on `reopen`.\n- `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_claim_transition_parent_cascade.py` — reopen-cascade\n  and benign-race cases beside the existing claim/transition cases.\n- `tests/interfaces/store/test_completion_verifier.py` — rebuild the non-recursion test's\n  fixture state with `cascade=False`.\n- `docs/concurrency.md` — §I4a: both cascading edges and the benign-race re-check.\n\nAssume the no-rollback half of the fail-fast contract is correct and is not revisited here.\n\n## Testing\nHeld-out tests in `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_claim_transition_parent_cascade.py`,\nusing that file's existing `rebar_repo` fixture and `_status()` helper:\n- Happy path: reopen a closed child under a closed parent -> both `open`.\n- No-op: reopen a closed child whose parent is already `open` (and, separately, already\n  `in_progress`) -> the parent's status is untouched.\n- Multi-level: a closed grand/parent/child chain, reopen the leaf -> all three `open`.\n- Failure/edge: monkeypatch `txn.transition_core` to raise `ConcurrencyMismatch` for the\n  parent -> `rebar.reopen(child)` raises `ConcurrencyError` with `returncode == 10`, the\n  message names the parent, and the child is still `closed` (no partial cascade).\n- Benign race, both edges: monkeypatch `txn.transition_core` so the parent LEAVES the\n  eligible status and then raises -> the child still moves and the peer's write stands.\n- `cascade=False` suppression on the reopen edge.\n- Regression: `test_close_does_not_cascade_to_parent` keeps passing, and\n  `tests/interfaces/store/test_completion_verifier.py` passes.\nRun: `python -m pytest tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_claim_transition_parent_cascade.py\ntests/interfaces/store/test_completion_verifier.py`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] Reopening a child under a closed parent reopens that parent transitively to the\n      root, in one invocation, parent before child. Checked by the happy-path and\n      multi-level tests asserting every ancestor is `open` after one `rebar.reopen(leaf)`.\n- [x] No closed parent is left holding a non-closed child after a reopen. Checked by the\n      happy-path and multi-level tests asserting the parent is `open`, not `closed`.\n- [x] An already-`open` or already-`in_progress` parent is not disturbed by a reopen.\n      Checked by the two no-op tests.\n- [x] A raced parent surfaces `ConcurrencyError`/exit 10 naming the parent, with the child\n      left `closed`. Checked by the failure/edge test.\n- [x] The transition cascade treats a benign parent race exactly as `claim`'s cascade\n      does. Checked by the two benign-race tests asserting the child still moves.\n- [x] `docs/concurrency.md` §I4a describes the `closed -> open` cascade beside the\n      `open -> in_progress` one and no longer says `reopen` never cascades. Checked by\n      reading the updated §I4a.\n- [x] `make lint` and `make typecheck` pass, complexity ratchet reporting no `new` or\n      `increased` entries."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787179899028960001,"uuid":"c07c8d5d-8f36-40be-be73-b144ec905cc7"}